  <Fragment slot="lede">It's rarely that nobody cares. More often, acting on the research is harder than ignoring it. Lower that cost.</Fragment>

  <h2>The short answer</h2>

  <p>Research moves the roadmap when it feeds a decision someone is already making. Shape each insight so it's easy to act on: link it to a current goal, say why now, and show the evidence. Reward insight more than ideas, and the team starts trading in evidence. And plan for politics. Good research can sway a decision, but it can't route around the people who make it.</p>

  <h2>Research earns its keep in decisions</h2>

  <p>Discovery exists to make future decisions clearer. If it never changes what anyone does, nobody will value it, however good it is.</p>

  <p>Product leaders, designers and researchers tell me the same thing: plenty of research, not much action. I think of product work as <a href="/guides/product-decision-quality/">setting up the conditions for good decisions</a>. Research is one of the strongest tools we have for that.</p>

  <p>It won't make anyone certain. What it does is make us a bit less wrong, and since most of our bets start out wrong, being less wrong is worth a lot.</p>

  <h2>Shape insights for action</h2>

  <p>The insights that get used tend to share seven traits:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Relevant.</strong> They connect to a goal the company is already chasing.</li>
    <li><strong>Significant.</strong> They matter to this audience. A 1% shift is huge in one industry and noise in another.</li>
    <li><strong>Outcome oriented.</strong> They show what the business gets if it invests.</li>
    <li><strong>Timely.</strong> They make the case for acting now, rather than later or not at all.</li>
    <li><strong>Unbiased.</strong> You choose which findings to show, but you never bend them. Research informs the decision; people make it.</li>
    <li><strong>Contextual.</strong> They sit next to the decision in front of the room, not three slides in.</li>
    <li><strong>Specific.</strong> They carry the numbers, the quotes and enough detail to be believed.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>An invented example, for a food delivery app. The weak version:</p>

  <blockquote><p>Customers find our delivery times unreliable.</p></blockquote>

  <p>The version that gets acted on:</p>

  <blockquote><p>This quarter we're trying to lift our app store rating. Over the last 90 days, four in five one-star reviews blame delivery times. Fixing delivery time estimates is one of the most direct routes to that goal.</p></blockquote>

  <p>Same finding. The second one names the goal, brings the evidence and says why it matters this quarter.</p>

  <h2>Reward insight over ideas</h2>

  <p>Teams learn fast what their leader gets excited about. Light up at ideas, and you'll get a stream of ideas. Light up at evidence, and you'll get evidence. Keep ideas cheap and give the problem most of the airtime.</p>

  <p>Features and benefits are easy to confuse. Let the findings lead the conversation instead. Invite stakeholders into the research itself, not just the readout. And if you can't trace a decision back to evidence, it was probably a hunch.</p>

  <h2>Show that research matters to you</h2>

  <p>Few things signal priority like a product leader sitting in on a research interview. I block out a day now and then to help a team run sessions. It keeps my own judgement grounded, and the team sees that I'm not asking them to do something I won't.</p>

  <p>Make time for your researchers. Help clear what's blocking them. And when research shaped a decision, say so, out loud and by name.</p>

  <h2>Plan for politics</h2>

  <p><a href="/writing/impact-cant-bypass-politics/">Evidence can influence politics</a>. It can't skip them. A finding reads slightly differently to everyone in the room.</p>

  <p>When a favourite feature takes over the conversation, ask how it gets you to the outcome everyone agreed on. If the link is assumed, walk through it step by step. Get agreement on the problem before anyone debates the fix.</p>

  <h2>When to bring someone in</h2>

  <p>If research keeps ending up in decks instead of decisions, or nobody senior owns how evidence reaches the roadmap, that's a product leadership gap. A <a href="/fractional/">fractional product leader</a> can set up the path from insight to decision, then hand it to the team.</p>

